  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the step function using a combination of evolutionary strategies and constrained optimization, leveraging symmetry to reduce dimensionality while ensuring feasibility. We overcome previous methods' limitations by focusing on search efficiency through adaptive ensembles of optimization techniques and probabilistic repair mechanisms. This enhances exploration without compromising the continuous improvement approach.\n\nMathematical Specification\n- Representation: Discrete samples at n points (even-sized grid) over [0,2]\n- Independent variables: Symmetric part of h, represented by z (length m=n_samples//2)\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (convolution with finite support)\n- Constraints: \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (handled by projection), h_i \u2208 [0,1] (ensured via repair)\n- Objective approximation: Softmax log-sum-exp for stability\n- Solver interfaces: SLSQP/COBYLA for local refinement, with repair-based soft constraints\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Generate multiple diverse starting points using:\n     * Upsampled parent with repair projection\n     * Probabilistic boundary search (corner trim strips)\n     * Simplex/Discrete simplex initialization\n   - For each start, project integral constraint via densest feasible point\n\n2. Optimization Phase:\n   - Dual-stage local refinement with adaptive barrier parameters\n   - Primary stage: Soft constraint via D-iteration (abandon if violated)\n   - Secondary stage: Tighter bounds with volume preservation repair\n   - Statistical monitoring: Early exit if convergence stalls\n\n3. Candidate Triage:\n   - Post-hoc repair of boundary violators using penalty-driven repair\n   - Multilevel acceptance criteria: hierarchical score reduction\n   - Time-based lottery selection for final candidate\n\n4. Repair Mechanisms:\n   - Violation detection via sliding window histogram\n   - Bounded repair: Min movement toward feasible region\n   - Volume/perturbation strategy: Sample near violated region\n\n5. Feasibility Packets:\n   - Maintain multidimensional archive of top candidates\n   - Update dynamically: Refine repair when persistence threshold met\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- Population sizing: 4-12 strategies based on niche diversity\n- Repair variance: Dynamically adjust sterilization window [0.85,0.97]\n- Objective weighting: Adaptive alpha sweep [20,45] in (24)\n- Stage switching: Triggered by divergence in objective improvement\n\nBudget and Robustness\n- Time allocation: 70% initial seeding, 30% refinement\n- Dimensionality control: Symmetric representation reduces n by factor ~2\n- Batch healing: Repair fallback component applied to top 10%\n- Progressive cost: Complexity O(n log n/m) due to adaptive repair\n\nValidation and Contract\n1. Post-processing validation:\n   - Confirm integral via high-precision recomputation\n   - Boundary check with binary detector\n   - Reevaluate c5 using correlated Cauchy approximation\n2. Raise exception if repair fails 5 iterations\n3. Fallback: Deep repair ensemble if local optima detected\n4. Ensure minimal adaptation through repair control thresholds",
